  <a href="#section-spring-paths" id="section-spring-paths" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Choose a Spring integration path</h2>

  <section>
    <div class="wide">
      <p>
        The same checkout service can send test receipts to a local SMTP inbox and use an authenticated relay in production.
        Put the SMTP server, credentials, transport strategy, and defaults in Spring configuration, then inject the same Mailer in both profiles.
      </p>
      <p>
		Spring integration lives in <code class="inline language-text">spring-module</code>. The Spring Boot starter adds that module. It declares Boot,
		Spring Framework, and SLF4J as provided dependencies, so the application gets their versions from its Boot parent or BOM.
      </p>
      <div class="doc-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scrollable Spring integration paths">
        <table>
          <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Application path</th>
            <th>Dependency</th>
            <th>Activation</th>
          </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Spring Boot 2.7 or 3.x &mdash; recommended</td>
            <td><code class="inline language-text">simple-java-mail-spring-boot-starter</code>, which brings in <code class="inline language-text">spring-module</code></td>
            <td>Automatic</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Spring Boot already using the module directly</td>
            <td><code class="inline language-text">spring-module</code></td>
            <td>Automatic</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Plain Spring</td>
            <td><code class="inline language-text">spring-module</code></td>
            <td><code class="inline language-java">@Import(SimpleJavaMailSpringSupport.class)</code></td>
          </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
		Every route exposes the same context-local <code class="inline language-java">SimpleJavaMailConfig</code>,
		<code class="inline language-java">SimpleJavaMail</code>, and default <code class="inline language-java">Mailer</code> beans. There is no shared builder
		bean; inject the factory whenever you need a fresh builder.
      </p>
      <p>
		The <code class="inline language-properties">spring.mail.*</code> namespace and <code class="inline language-java">JavaMailSender</code> belong to Spring
		Mail, not this integration. Configure Simple Java Mail through <code class="inline language-properties">simplejavamail.*</code>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="view">
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Which profile wins?</strong> Spring resolves profiles, placeholders, environment variables, and system properties using its own
          precedence. All three paths load <code class="inline language-text">simplejavamail.properties</code> as a low-priority source. The factory
          captures the resolved values from Spring's <code class="inline language-java">Environment</code> without rereading the process environment.</li>
        <li><strong>What if Java also sets the relay?</strong> The <a href="/understanding-configuration.html#connection-conflicts">connection conflict policy</a>
          checks explicit connection declarations after Spring has settled on a value for each property. Two distinct values for the same connection
          setting fail by default. Equivalent parsed values are fine. See <a href="/understanding-configuration.html#configuration-resolution-timing">when choices take effect</a>
          for how captured values, Java choices, and Email templates fit together.</li>
        <li><strong>What if a test starts another context?</strong> Each context has its own snapshot and factory. Building or closing a Mailer in one
          context leaves the other context's settings alone.</li>
        <li><strong>What if you omit the relay?</strong> Spring still starts. Sending then uses <code class="inline language-text">localhost</code> by default,
          so set <code class="inline language-properties">simplejavamail.smtp.host</code> wherever you send without a local SMTP server.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-spring-versions" id="section-spring-versions" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Match the application's Spring Boot line</h2>

  <section>
    <div class="wide">
      <p>
		The same Simple Java Mail starter supports both Boot generations. Add it to an existing Boot application; the application's parent or BOM supplies
		the Boot, Spring Framework, and SLF4J versions.
      </p>
      <div class="doc-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scrollable Spring Boot compatibility matrix">
        <table>
          <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Application line</th>
            <th>Application-supplied stack</th>
            <th>Compatibility checks</th>
          </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Spring Boot 2.7.x</td>
            <td>Spring Framework 5.3 and SLF4J 1.7</td>
            <td>Boot 2.7.18 on Java 11</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Spring Boot 3.x</td>
            <td>Spring Framework 6 and SLF4J 2</td>
            <td>Boot 3.0.13 on Java 17 and Boot 3.5.16 on Java 21</td>
          </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
		The checked versions are compatibility boundaries, not versions pulled into the application. Simple Java Mail keeps its own Java 11 bytecode
		baseline; the application must still meet the Java requirement of its chosen Boot line.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-spring-installation" id="section-spring-installation" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Install and inject the Spring beans</h2>

  <section>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
      In an application that already declares Spring Boot through its parent or BOM, add the Simple Java Mail starter with Maven:
      <pre><code class="language-xml">&lt;dependency&gt;
    &lt;groupId&gt;org.simplejavamail&lt;/groupId&gt;
    &lt;artifactId&gt;simple-java-mail-spring-boot-starter&lt;/artifactId&gt;
    &lt;version&gt;{{site.version}}&lt;/version&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;</code></pre>
      Or with Gradle:
      <pre><code class="language-groovy">implementation("org.simplejavamail:simple-java-mail-spring-boot-starter:{{site.version}}")</code></pre>
	  In plain Spring, activate the same bean path explicitly:
	  <pre><code class="small">@Configuration
@Import(SimpleJavaMailSpringSupport.class)
class SimpleJavaMailConfiguration {
}</code></pre>
      Then inject the default Mailer directly; Boot needs no <code class="inline language-java">@Import</code>:
      <pre><code class="small">@Component
public class YourEmailService {

    private final Mailer mailer;

    public YourEmailService(Mailer mailer) {
        this.mailer = mailer;
    }
}
</code></pre>
      Inject the factory when the service builds emails or needs a fresh Mailer builder:
      <pre><code class="small">@Component
public class YourEmailService {

    private final SimpleJavaMail mail;

    public YourEmailService(SimpleJavaMail mail) {
        this.mail = mail;
    }

    public Email newStatusEmail() {
        return mail.emailBuilder().startingBlank()
                .withSubject("Status")
                .buildEmail();
    }
}</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-spring-beans" id="section-spring-beans" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Customize Spring-managed beans</h2>

  <section>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
      <p>Say your notification worker needs a shorter idle timeout for its async threads and a specific port for its SOCKS bridge.
        Declare a <code class="inline language-java">Mailer</code> bean and build it from the injected factory so it still uses your Spring settings.</p>
      <pre><code class="small">@Configuration
public class MailConfiguration {

    @Bean(destroyMethod = "close")
    public Mailer customMailer(SimpleJavaMail mail) {
        return mail.mailerBuilder()
                .withAsyncExecutionConfig(AsyncExecutionConfig.builder()
                    .resetThreadPoolSize()
                    .withKeepAliveTime(Duration.ofSeconds(5))
                    .build())
                .withProxyConfig(SocksProxyConfig.builder()
                    .withBridgePort(7777)
                    .build())
                .buildMailer();
    }
}
</code></pre>
      <p>If Spring also declares the bridge port, the two values must agree by default. When you mean for the bean to override an explicit deployment
        value, use <code>withConnectionConflictPolicy(ConnectionConflictPolicy.ALLOW_EXPLICIT_OVERRIDES)</code> or the property below.
        Settings the bean leaves alone still come from the applicable imported values.</p>
      <pre><code class="language-yaml small">simplejavamail:
  connection:
    conflictpolicy: ALLOW_EXPLICIT_OVERRIDES</code></pre>
      <p>Use <code>simplejavamail.locked.connection.conflictpolicy=REJECT_CONFLICTS</code> when the deployment requires strict handling.
        Existing value locks and mandatory transport rules remain binding in both modes.</p>
      <p>Boot supplies each of the three beans independently. Choose the bean to replace based on what you need to change.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><code class="inline language-java">SimpleJavaMailConfig</code> supplies settings to the default factory and its Mailer.</li>
        <li><code class="inline language-java">SimpleJavaMail</code> replaces the factory the default Mailer uses.</li>
        <li><code class="inline language-java">Mailer</code> replaces only the sending bean. You can still inject the default config and factory.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Spring closes the default Mailer. For your own Mailer bean, Spring uses the declared or inferred destroy method. Set
        <code class="inline language-java">@Bean(destroyMethod = "")</code> only if you close the Mailer elsewhere.</p>
      <p>
        To disable all three defaults, use Spring Boot's standard exclusion:
      </p>
      <pre><code class="language-properties">spring.autoconfigure.exclude=org.simplejavamail.springsupport.SimpleJavaMailAutoConfiguration</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

	<a href="#section-spring-oauth2" id="section-spring-oauth2" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
	<h2>Provide OAuth2 access tokens through Spring</h2>

	<section>
		<div class="wide full-width-example">
			<p>
				When the default Mailer uses <code class="inline language-java">SMTP_OAUTH2</code> (required STARTTLS) or
				<code class="inline language-java">SMTPS_OAUTH2</code> (implicit TLS), a single
				<code class="inline language-java">OAuth2AccessTokenProvider</code> bean is picked up automatically:
			</p>
			<pre><code class="small">@Bean
OAuth2AccessTokenProvider smtpAccessTokens(TokenService tokenService) {
    return tokenService::currentAccessToken;
}</code></pre>
			<p>
				If the application has more than one <code class="inline language-java">OAuth2AccessTokenProvider</code> bean, mark the one for SMTP with
				<code class="inline language-java">@Primary</code> or use normal Spring bean disambiguation.
			</p>
			<p>
				Applications using Spring Security OAuth2 Client can adapt its
				<a href="https://docs.spring.io/spring-security/reference/servlet/oauth2/client/core.html">OAuth2AuthorizedClientManager</a>. The manager obtains
				or refreshes the authorized client when needed; Simple Java Mail receives only the current token value.
			</p>
			<pre><code class="small">@Bean
OAuth2AccessTokenProvider smtpAccessTokens(
        OAuth2AuthorizedClientManager authorizedClients) {
    return () -&gt; {
        OAuth2AuthorizeRequest request = OAuth2AuthorizeRequest
                .withClientRegistrationId("smtp")
                .principal("simple-java-mail")
                .build();

        OAuth2AuthorizedClient client = authorizedClients.authorize(request);
        if (client == null) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Unable to authorize the SMTP OAuth2 client");
        }
        return client.getAccessToken().getTokenValue();
    };
}</code></pre>
			<p>
				For scheduled jobs and other code without an HTTP request, Spring Security recommends an
				<code class="inline language-java">AuthorizedClientServiceOAuth2AuthorizedClientManager</code>. This adapter stays in your application; the Simple
				Java Mail Spring module adds no Spring Security dependency.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

  <a href="#section-spring-sending-limits" id="section-spring-sending-limits" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Configure sending limits</h2>
  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>If your relay lets one account send 30 messages and address 100 recipients per minute, put both limits next to its SMTP settings.
        Once configured, both caps apply. <code>allowbursts: false</code> also spaces out send starts within the rolling window.</p>
      <p>Mailers built from the injected <code>SimpleJavaMail</code> factory can share a named group's allowance when their rules agree.
        Constructing another factory, starting another application instance, or restarting this one starts independent history.
        See <a href="sending-and-execution.html#section-sending-limits">sending limits</a> for accounting, resets, clustering, and shutdown behavior.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-yaml small">simplejavamail:
  smtp:
    ratelimit:
      group: company-account
      messages:
        limit: 30
        period: PT1M
      recipients:
        limit: 100
        period: PT1M
      allowbursts: false</code></pre>
      <p>Limits are off until you configure them. Plain Spring and the Boot starter both read <code>simplejavamail.smtp.ratelimit.</code> settings,
        and Spring metadata provides IDE hints.
        Use the factory's <code>getConfig().getDiagnostics()</code> to trace loaded values and sources;
        <code>mailer.getOperationalConfig()</code> includes later Java builder choices.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-spring-import-limits" id="section-spring-import-limits" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Configure received-mail import limits</h2>
  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>A support inbox may receive forwarded mail inside a forwarded message. Set import limits to cap how much data each conversion can read
        and how deep it can go. The allowance covers the selected nested messages in that import. The next import starts with a fresh allowance.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Plain Spring and the Boot starter both capture these defaults for the injected <code>SimpleJavaMail</code> factory. Without a configured value,
          the library falls back to unlimited.</li>
        <li>Give an individual conversion an <code>ImportLimitsConfig</code> to change its limits. Omitted fields inherit factory settings and zero explicitly
          requests unlimited. See <a href="/email-conversion.html#conversion-import-limits">the Java API and result handling</a>.</li>
        <li>To enforce a limit, use <code>simplejavamail.locked.conversion.import.</code>. An explicit match is fine. A conflicting value, including an unlimited
          reset, fails before the import reads any input. Spring metadata supplies IDE hints for both namespaces.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="side">
      <pre><code class="language-yaml small">simplejavamail:
  conversion:
    import:
      maximuminputbytes: 26214400
      maximumpartcount: 1000
      maximumnestingdepth: 30</code></pre>
      <p>The input-size choice applies to raw EML/MSG sources. A supplied <code>MimeMessage</code> cannot establish its original encoded size;
        use raw input, or explicitly set/reset <code>maximumInputBytes</code> to zero on the limits builder (subject to locks) while retaining
        part/depth choices. <code>getConfig().getDiagnostics()</code> shows captured values and sources.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-spring-smime-cache" id="section-spring-smime-cache" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Choose S/MIME key reuse capacity</h2>
  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>If your app signs mail with a different S/MIME key for each customer, set how many loaded key configurations it can keep in memory.
        Mailers and converters from the injected factory share that cache.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><code>-1</code> (the default) keeps an unlimited number of entries.</li>
        <li><code>0</code> keeps no completed entries.</li>
        <li>A positive integer caps the number of entries and evicts the least recently used when the cache fills.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Use <code>simplejavamail.locked.smime.cache.capacity</code> when the captured value must remain fixed. This setting belongs to the
        factory root; named Mailer definitions cannot override it. Separately configured factories have independent reuse.</p>
      <p>Changing an Environment value does not reconfigure existing objects. Create a replacement configured factory and its Mailers and
        converters to use another capacity. Closing one Mailer releases its sending resources and leaves other participants' shared keys
        available. See <a href="security.html#section-smime-key-cache">capacity values, Java usage and lifetime</a>.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="side">
      <pre><code class="language-yaml small">simplejavamail:
  smime:
    cache:
      capacity: 10000</code></pre>
      <p>This example caches up to 10,000 key configurations. Entries have no time-based expiry. Configure signing and decryption separately.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-spring-recipient-rejection" id="section-spring-recipient-rejection" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Choose how recipient rejections are handled</h2>
  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>If an ops alert goes to three people and one address is rejected, you may still want the other two to get it. Set this default in plain Spring or
        Spring Boot to send to the accepted recipients. The operation still reports partial acceptance as a failure and includes the receipt.</p>
      <p>To withhold the message from everyone when the server rejects any recipient, set
        <code>SmtpDeliveryOptions.Builder.withSendingToAcceptedRecipients(false)</code>. Clearing the per-message choice restores the default.</p>
      <p>To keep the choice fixed within the injected factory, move the property under <code>locked.defaults</code> instead.
        For example, <code>simplejavamail.locked.defaults.sendtoacceptedrecipients=false</code> requires stopping on known recipient rejection.
        Equal explicit choices are allowed; conflicting ones fail. See the
        <a href="analyzing-send-results.html#section-partial-send">complete walkthrough and provider compatibility</a>.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-yaml small">simplejavamail:
  defaults:
    sendtoacceptedrecipients: true</code></pre>
      <p>Omitted means no configured choice. The shared loader captures the Spring value; later Java and Email choices follow the
        <a href="/understanding-configuration.html#configuration-precedence">setting's resolution rules</a>. Spring metadata supplies IDE hints.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <p class="wide">The shared Environment loader also supports <a href="/smtp-connection-failover.html#section-named">named failover Mailers</a>,
      their policies and <code>simplejavamail.locked.*</code> counterparts. Use the same property names in properties files or nested YAML.
      Each named definition keeps its own sources and settings; it does not inherit the default Mailer's ordinary properties.</p>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-spring-locked-configuration" id="section-spring-locked-configuration" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Keep selected settings fixed</h2>
  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>Suppose every invoice must go through your company relay over STARTTLS and reach the archive mailbox, even when a team supplies its own Mailer.
        Put the relay, transport strategy, and archive BCC in <a href="configuration.html#section-locked-configuration">locked settings</a>.
        Any Mailer built from the injected factory keeps them. The BCC applies to the actual delivery envelope.</p>
      <p>Keep the port in ordinary settings if teams may need to change it. An explicit Java override still needs the connection conflict policy shown above.</p>
      <p>The shared loader reads both namespaces through Spring's <code>Environment</code>, including profiles, aliases and placeholders.
        Diagnostics name the underlying source that declared the property. IDE hints reuse the ordinary property types; there are no wrapped
        <code>value</code>/<code>locked</code> objects and no second runtime binder.</p>
      <p>Build your Mailers from the injected factory to pick up these locks. An independent factory uses its own configuration.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-yaml small">simplejavamail:
  smtp:
    port: 587
  locked:
    smtp:
      host: relay.company.com
    transportstrategy: SMTP_TLS
    defaults:
      bcc:
        address: archive@company.com</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

	<a href="#section-spring-profiles" id="section-spring-profiles" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
	<h2>Configure profiles and dynamic properties</h2>

  <section>

    <div class="wide">
      <p>Use the default profile to send to your test server and see the SMTP transcript. Activate <code>production</code>
        to switch to an authenticated relay behind a proxy and turn the transcript off.</p>
      <pre><code class="language-properties small">#application.properties
simplejavamail.javaxmail.debug=true
simplejavamail.smtp.host=smtp.host
simplejavamail.smtp.port=25
simplejavamail.transportstrategy=SMTP
</code></pre>
      <pre><code class="language-properties small">#application-production.properties
simplejavamail.javaxmail.debug=false
simplejavamail.smtp.host=smtp.production.host
simplejavamail.smtp.port=465
simplejavamail.transportstrategy=SMTPS
simplejavamail.smtp.username=&lt;username&gt;
simplejavamail.smtp.password=&lt;password&gt;
simplejavamail.proxy.host=proxy.production.host
simplejavamail.proxy.port=1080
simplejavamail.proxy.username=&lt;proxy_username&gt;
simplejavamail.proxy.password=&lt;proxy_password&gt;
</code></pre>
      <p>If your order emails have a dedicated pool, tune that cluster independently. Use a readable name and supply its UUID, or use the UUID directly.
        Spring uses the same per-cluster property names as a standalone properties file.</p>
      <pre><code class="language-properties small">#application.properties
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.orders.clusterkey.uuid=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000301
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.orders.coresize=0
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.orders.maxsize=3
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.orders.claimtimeout.millis=30000
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.orders.expireafter.millis=600000
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.orders.loadbalancing.strategy=RANDOM_ACCESS

# or key the config directly by UUID
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000302.maxsize=8
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000302.loadbalancing.strategy=ROUND_ROBIN
</code></pre>
      <p>If one deployment has a verified route that requires the
        <a href="/sending-and-execution.html#section-international-mail">legacy SMTP content opt-in</a>, enable the Boolean only in that profile.
        Verify the server and onward route first. TLS requirements still apply, and the setting gives no evidence of downstream delivery.</p>
<pre><code class="language-properties small"># application-verified-legacy-relay.properties
simplejavamail.smtp.legacycontentsupport=true</code></pre>
	</div>
  </section>
  <section>
	<div class="view">
      <p>Your IDE can suggest <code class="inline language-properties">simplejavamail.*</code> properties and show their types in
        <code class="inline language-text">application.properties</code> and YAML. At startup, Spring's <code class="inline language-java">Environment</code>
        still resolves the actual values.</p>
      <p>You'll get suggestions for dynamic prefixes such as <code class="inline language-properties">extraproperties.*</code> and connection-pool
        clusters. You supply the child keys, such as the cluster name. Some properties have generated aliases, like those shown here, for names
        Spring's relaxed binding cannot represent cleanly.</p>
	</div>

	<div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-properties small">simplejavamail.javaxmail.debug-out=SLF4J
simplejavamail.custom.sslfactory.clazz=org.mypackage.ssl.MySSLSocketFactoryClass
simplejavamail.defaults.poolsize-more.keepalivetime=2000
simplejavamail.smime.signing.keystore-password=keystore_password
simplejavamail.smime.signing.key-alias=key_alias
simplejavamail.smime.signing.key-password=key_password
simplejavamail.dkim.signing.private-key-file-or-data=file:my_dkim_key.der
simplejavamail.dkim.signing.signing-domain=your-domain.com
# Leave header exclusions unset unless a known relay rewrites a header; From must remain signed.
</code></pre>
	</div>
  </section>

  <section><p class="wide">Configure <a href="/mail-send-retries.html#section-configuration">mail-send retries</a> through the same Environment path:
    <code>simplejavamail.smtp.retry.attempts</code> and <code>simplejavamail.smtp.retry.delay</code> are both required. Backoff, jitter and
    <code>simplejavamail.defaults.mailsend.retry.*</code> resource limits have ordinary and locked IDE metadata. Named Mailers resolve their own schedules;
    they do not inherit the initiating Mailer's policy. These are Java callback-free settings, so CLI configuration is supported too.</p></section>

  <a href="#section-spring-telemetry" id="section-spring-telemetry" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Enable mail telemetry</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>With the <a href="/opentelemetry.html#section-register">optional OpenTelemetry module</a> available, enable telemetry through configuration:</p>
    <pre><code class="language-properties">simplejavamail.telemetry.enabled=true</code></pre>
    <p>Or enable it in Java on a configuration class in an application already using the Spring integration:</p>
    <pre><code class="language-java">import org.simplejavamail.springsupport.EnableSjmTelemetry;

@Configuration
@EnableSjmTelemetry
class MailTelemetryConfiguration {
    @Bean
    MailSendTelemetry mailTelemetry(OpenTelemetry openTelemetry) {
        return OtelAdapter.wrap(openTelemetry);
    }
}</code></pre>
    <p>The annotation enables telemetry without requiring an enabled property. The bean independently selects the integration for Spring's
      default Mailer; a bean alone does not enable telemetry. An arbitrary OpenTelemetry bean is not discovered by SJM.
      If several <code>MailSendTelemetry</code> beans exist, select a primary one.</p>
    <p>Both activation routes apply to every Mailer from the configured factory. Enabled Mailers without a supplied integration use the
      application's global OpenTelemetry instance. Fresh builders do not inherit the default Mailer's integration bean; supply the integration
      through <code>withTelemetryConfig(...)</code> when those builders should use it too.</p>
    <p>Explicit <code>simplejavamail.telemetry.enabled</code> properties override the annotation, so a deployment can set it to
      <code>false</code>. Locked values remain authoritative. Endpoint permission uses <code>simplejavamail.telemetry.smtp.endpointattributes</code>;
      activation does not grant it. These settings and their locked counterparts retain the same Environment resolution and Boolean IDE metadata.</p>
    <p>The annotation uses the existing integration: plain Spring still imports <code>SimpleJavaMailSpringSupport</code>, while Boot uses its
      existing auto-configuration. Application-provided configuration snapshots, factories and Mailers keep their own choices; the annotation
      does not rewrite replacement beans. Your application owns SDK/exporter setup and shutdown, including global initialization before
      constructing enabled Mailers. See the <a href="/opentelemetry.html#section-integration">OpenTelemetry integration guide</a> for the full wiring.</p>
